<quiz>
<question type="stack">
  <name><text>Single matrix for two successive transformations</text></name>
  <questiontext format="html">
    <text><![CDATA[<p>\(P = {@Pm@}\) and \(Q = {@Qm@}\). A shape is transformed by \(P\), and its image is then transformed by \(Q\).</p><p>(a) Find the single matrix that maps the shape straight onto its final image.</p><div>[[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]] [[feedback:prt1]]</div><p>(b) \(K({@k[1]@},\,{@k[2]@})\) is a vertex of the shape. Find its final image.</p><div>( [[input:ans2]] , [[input:ans3]] ) [[validation:ans2]] [[validation:ans3]] [[feedback:prt2]]</div>]]></text>
  </questiontext>
  <generalfeedback format="html">
    <text><![CDATA[<p>The matrix of the transformation done first is written on the right, next to the point it acts on. \(P\) is done first, so the single matrix is \(QP\):</p><p>\(QP = {@Qm@}{@Pm@} = {@ta@}\)</p><p>(b) \({@ta@}{@kc@} = {@ta.kc@}\), so the final image of K is \(({@tk[1]@},\,{@tk[2]@})\). Check in two steps: \(P\) sends K to \(({@k1[1]@},\,{@k1[2]@})\), and \(Q\) sends that point to \(({@tk[1]@},\,{@tk[2]@})\).</p>]]></text>
  </generalfeedback>
  <defaultgrade>2</defaultgrade>
  <penalty>0.1</penalty>
  <hidden>0</hidden>
  <idnumber></idnumber>
  <stackversion><text>2026063000</text></stackversion>
  <questionvariables>
    <text><![CDATA[validate_numeric(ex) := block([sx], sx: ev(ex, simp), if listofvars(ex) = [] and freeof(%i, sx) then "" else "Type a number in each box, not a letter.");
near(L,T) := is(abs(L[1]-T[1]) + abs(L[2]-T[2]) < 0.001);
/* Design: single matrix for "a followed by b". Source: adapted from SMP Book 5 (Metric), Chapter 14, Exercise B, question 4. PQ, the entrywise product and the sum differ from QP, and their images of K from each other and from the follow-through value. */
plist: [[matrix([-2,-3],[-3,-1]),matrix([-1,-3],[0,-1]),[3,-2]],[matrix([1,1],[-2,-1]),matrix([-2,1],[-3,3]),[2,1]],[matrix([-1,-3],[3,-3]),matrix([1,0],[3,-3]),[-2,3]],[matrix([-1,2],[1,1]),matrix([0,-1],[3,2]),[-1,1]],[matrix([1,-1],[-3,-3]),matrix([-3,2],[0,-3]),[-2,-1]],[matrix([-3,-1],[-3,-2]),matrix([-3,-1],[2,3]),[1,1]],[matrix([-2,2],[3,2]),matrix([0,1],[3,1]),[1,1]],[matrix([0,3],[-1,1]),matrix([2,-1],[-2,-1]),[2,3]],[matrix([1,1],[0,3]),matrix([1,2],[1,-1]),[1,1]],[matrix([3,-3],[-1,3]),matrix([1,1],[1,-2]),[-1,-2]]];
ix: 1+rand(10);
Pm: plist[ix][1]; Qm: plist[ix][2]; k: plist[ix][3];
ta: Qm.Pm;
kc: transpose(matrix(k));
tk: list_matrix_entries(ta.kc);
k1: list_matrix_entries(Pm.kc);
/* diagnostics: PQ; entrywise product; sum */
dPQ: Pm.Qm; dE: Pm*Qm; dS: Pm+Qm;
kPQ: list_matrix_entries(dPQ.kc); kE: list_matrix_entries(dE.kc);
/* tests: QP rebuilt entry by entry; generic offsets */
tr: matrix([Qm[1,1]*Pm[1,1]+Qm[1,2]*Pm[2,1], Qm[1,1]*Pm[1,2]+Qm[1,2]*Pm[2,2]],[Qm[2,1]*Pm[1,1]+Qm[2,2]*Pm[2,1], Qm[2,1]*Pm[1,2]+Qm[2,2]*Pm[2,2]]);
tw: ta+matrix([1,0],[0,0]); tkw: tk+[0,1];
/* tkw is clear of the follow-through value tw.K, which moves only the x-coordinate */
]]></text>
  </questionvariables>
